@h1 Iteration h1@

@@@code (each @collection as item) … (end) code@@@ renders its body once per
element, in source order. It is how you turn a collection into a table of
contents, a list, a set of cards — any repeated view of your data.

@h2 Over scalars and over records h2@

@@@pre
#themes: [ solitude, vigilance, return ]

(each @themes as item) The watch returned to @item. (end)
pre@@@

Inside the body, @@@code @item code@@@ is the whole element (fixture
13-iteration/each-over-values). When the elements are records, walk into them
with a dotted access:

@@@pre
#watchers: [ { name - Ada, post - lamp }, { name - Bo, post - log } ]

(each @watchers as item) @item.name kept the @item.post. (end)
pre@@@

(each-over-records.) Loop variables are usable inside parameter values too —
@@@code @watch |keeper @item.name| code@@@ (body-with-params).

@h2 Collections only h2@

The target must be a collection. A record or scalar is @strong not iterable
strong@ — @code E_NOT_ITERABLE code@. There is no "iterate a record's fields"
form; to loop a nested list, point at it with a dotted target like
@@@code @deck.hands code@@@.

@h2 Scope and shadowing h2@

The loop variable is @strong body-local strong@ and @strong shadows strong@ a
same-named global:

@@@pre
#item: solitary
#hands: [ { name - Ada }, { name - Bo } ]

Outside the loop, @item is solitary.

(each @hands as item) @item.name (end)
pre@@@

Outside, @@@code @item code@@@ is the global @code solitary code@; inside, it is
each element in turn (verified; fixture item-name-shadowing). Resolution
precedence is: iteration frames (innermost first), then data definitions, then
node definitions.

@h2 What iteration does not give you h2@

There is @strong no index, position, first, last, or counter strong@ variable —
call it out when you plan a layout that needs numbering, and supply the numbers
in the data or via a script. An @strong empty collection emits nothing strong@
(not an error; fixture empty-collection), an empty body is legal (empty-body),
and order is always preserved (iteration-order-preservation).

@h2 Nesting and combining with conditions h2@

Loops nest — the inner loop gets its own frame, and the outer element stays
visible:

@@@pre
(each @decks as deck)
  (each @deck.hands as hand) @hand, (end)
(end)
pre@@@

(nested-each; @@@code (end) code@@@ tokens pair last-in-first-out, end-token-pairing.)
A loop and a condition compose, with the condition resolved through the
iteration frame:

@@@pre
(each @hands as item)
  (if @item.awake is true) @item.name is on watch. (end)
(end)
pre@@@

(verified; fixture each-with-if.)

@h2 Common mistakes h2@

@ul
@li Iterating a record — use a collection, or a dotted list inside it. li@
@li Expecting an index variable — there is none. li@
@li Gluing @@@code (each …)(if …) code@@@ with no space — the inner statement is not
recognised. Keep a space or newline (see Gotchas). li@
ul@

